             Animation on the Series3

"Flicker" is an app for displaying a series of PIC files in sequence. It is
based upon FLICK.OPA, as written by Mark Emery (or should it be Emerya?) which
I have classed as version 1.0 in the history list.

"Flicker" is also a really good novel by Theodore Roszak!

As this program is based upon somebody else's work I couldn't, in all honesty,
ask for any recompense. What I do ask is that you copy and distribute this
program as widely as possibly. Also, if you come up with any suggestions for
improvements then please mail them to me and, if used, I will credit you in
the history list. Of course, I've got full copyright on this program!

To set up the application to run on the Psion Series 3 you should copy
flicker.app into your \APP\ directory and the sample animation DANCER01.PIC
to DANCER11.PIC should go into a \FLICK\ directory.

As Mark says, in his original documentation:
"The Dancer frames come from a Mac DA of the same name. I was given no
docs for the Mac version so can't give credit to the chap that did the
graphics. Who ever it was thanks. I only used them because they were the
right size and could be pulled out of the DA with ResEdit faster than I
could set up the digitiser. If anyone can tell me who the person is I'd
like to know." And that goes for me too!


History:

Version 1.0
24/05/92 - Changed .PIC file names to end in 2 digit number, ie 01,02 etc.
         - "Dancer" will now look for files in the \PIC\ directory or the
           drive it is running from, then at the other two internal drives.
         - "Flick" now prompts the user with a file name box. You should
           tell it which *01.PIC file, ie DANCER01.PIC, you want to start
           with - just in case you have lots of animations.
        When the file box is shown, select the drive you want to read
           from and press TAB to get a full screen file select box.
         - The frame number in "Flick" is no longer hard coded. It will
           now search to see how many frames it can find.

Version 2.0
23/08/93 - Rewritten into C and released.

I can be contacted through the Psion Echo on your local BBS.

Have fun and please tell me what you think!

Keith Jones

